Topi Linkala wrote:
> Derek Ray wrote:
> 
>> Turning your own arguments right around on you:  who are you to declare
>> that scripting is bad?  What if some people enjoy the challenge of
>> scripting a roguelike?  What's wrong with that?  Why are you out to
>> destroy those scripters' fun and mandate that something they enjoy doing
>> _must_ be boring?
> 
> If farming is bad because of scripting then let's abolish scripting not 
> farming.

Scripting is the mechanical or automated task of something that is often
monotoneous and doesn't require flexibility in response. I don't see why
it would be necessary to abolish scripting. Anyone?

> If scripting is capable on making the game easy why don't you write the 
> NetHack robot. No-one has done that, so you could actually show us that 
> farming is bad.

Scripting is simplifying the effort to re-type exact patterns of actions
based on rules into a machine. The game is not easier through scripting,
just data input is easier.

But an "element" of the game that is so easily possible to be performed
or solved by scripting is a broken game feature if it provides a practical
solution that replaces problem solving.

> I say that scripting is bad because it's not _playing_ the game.

If "playing the game" is mostly just the process of data input you are
right. If, OTOH, it is the problem solving then rather the opposite is
true; scripting is not bad, rather the specific scriptable game element
is broken.

> I can undesrtand the challenge to write a script that could play 
> NetHack, but I cannot see the joy in using it.

There are two applications of scripting mixed here in the argumentation;
a script playing a complete game (an expert system), and having a script
do the stupid task of taking over non-challenging game mechanics (e.g.
while exploiting flaws that can be automatized by scripts).

Nethack is a very hard "scriptable" game. A few, very few, features are
scriptable, at least a couple of them are under suspicion to be broken.

